name":"Bakersfield Sound","n":1},{"id":31379669920904,"type":"genre","name":"Traditional Country","n":1},{"id":7374429919951,"type":"genre","name":"country","n":1}],"labels":[],"album_labels":[],"own_scenes":[],"scenes":[],"book_tracks":[],"mentions":[],"podcast":[{"kind":"podcast","title":"First Recording Session (1958)","body":"Buddy Holly produced Waylon Jennings's very first recording session in September 1958, resulting in an interesting blend of Cajun and R&B music on the song \"Joli Blanc.\" \"In September he produced a session for his friend Whale and Jennings, who would later become a huge country star.\"","year":1958,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"A History of Rock Music in 500 Songs","episode":"Episode 74: \"It Doesn't Matter Any More\" by Buddy Holly","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6unib5T2mDQ"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Early Association with Buddy Holly (1959)","body":"Waylon Jennings briefly played in Buddy Holly's band in 1959 before going on to achieve his own major successes as a famous musician from Lubbock, Texas. \"Other than Buddy Holly, the two most famous people from Lubbock are probably Whale and Jennings, who briefly played in Holly's band in 1959, before going on to his own major successes.\"","year":1959,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"A History of Rock Music in 500 Songs","episode":"Episode Sixty-One: \"That'll Be the Day\", by the Crickets","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KVJMKLc54O0"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Plane Seat Incident (1959)","body":"On the \"Winter Dance Party\" tour, Waylon Jennings gave up his seat on Buddy Holly's chartered plane to The Big Bopper, who was ill. Jennings jokingly told Holly, \"I hope your old boss freezes,\" to which Holly retorted, \"yeah well I hope you are all playing crashes.\" This exchange tragically preceded the plane crash that killed Holly. \"Jennings replied, yeah well I hope you are all playing crashes.\"","year":1959,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"A History of Rock Music in 500 Songs","episode":"Episode 74: \"It Doesn't Matter Any More\" by Buddy Holly","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6unib5T2mDQ"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Winter Dance Party Bassist (1959)","body":"Waylon Jennings joined Buddy Holly's new touring band as the bassist for the \"Winter Dance Party\" tour, despite having never played bass before the tour started. \"Whale and Jennings on bass, who had never played bass before starting the tour.\"","year":1959,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"A History of Rock Music in 500 Songs","episode":"Episode 74: \"It Doesn't Matter Any More\" by Buddy Holly","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6unib5T2mDQ"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Emmylou Harris's Grief","body":"Waylon Jennings is one of the beloved musicians that Emmylou Harris has struggled to accept as gone, illustrating the close-knit community and personal connections within the music world. \"Even now, she has said it is hard to believe for her that so many of the people that were in her life and that she loved to hear sing and sing with wail and Jennings and towns van Zant just to name a couple that they're gone.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 35 - Cosmic American Music - 70s Country Rock","url":null},{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 35 - Cosmic American Music - 70s Country Rock","url":"https://seventies.libsyn.com/ep-35-cosmic-american-music-70s-country-rock"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Intimate Showcase at The Troubadour","body":"Waylon Jennings performed at The Troubadour, utilizing the venue to present an intimate show. This offered fans and writers an up-close look at his artistry, showcasing the club's role in providing a more personal performance experience for established artists. \"So if you saw someone like Well and Jennings here, you know, it was an attempt to get an intimate show for an audience of, you know, a showcase, you know, for an audience of fans and writers and get an up close look.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 41 - The Impact of The Troubadour on 1970s Popular Culture","url":null},{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 41 - The Impact of The Troubadour on 1970s Popular Culture","url":"https://seventies.libsyn.com/ep-41-the-impact-of-the-troubadour-on-1970s-popular-culture"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Labeled as \"Hippies\"","body":"Waylon Jennings, along with Willie Nelson, faced criticism and skepticism for injecting rock elements into their country sound, with some questioning their authenticity by labeling them as \"hippies.\" He was also in the midst of the most commercially successful period of his career when he performed at Billboard's International Radio Programming Forum in 1975. \"Why were they trying to be hippies?\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 35 - Cosmic American Music - 70s Country Rock","url":null},{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 35 - Cosmic American Music - 70s Country Rock","url":"https://seventies.libsyn.com/ep-35-cosmic-american-music-70s-country-rock"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Musical Parent","body":"Waylon Jennings is noted as the father of Shooter Jennings. \"shooter Jennings, son of Whalen.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"Musical Offspring","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MmXDbXI6Sqk"}]}],"awards":[{"kind":"award","title":"Grammy Award for Best Country Performance by a Duo or Group with Vocal (1970) — MacArthur Park","body":"Nominated.","year":1970,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Wikidata","episode":null,"url":"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q5593784"}]},{"kind":"award","title":"Grammy Award for Best Country Performance by a Duo or Group with Vocal (1979)","body":"Nominated.","year":1979,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Wikidata","episode":null,"url":"https://www.wikidata.org/wi